# .host-remap — the rename dictionary for the naming audit.
#
# `old => new` per line; the tool applies the substitutions literally and
# longest-`old`-first, and coins nothing itself: every `new` below is
# content the milestone already names its sections by.
#
# Why the milestone's own section numbers are here: the naming audit flags a
# dotted numeral and the phrase "section N" as tells, and host-lint refuses to
# sanction them ('section 26.15' carries the position noun 'section' as a word,
# so masking it would blank that token out of a real tell), naming the remedy
# as a rename. The plan's subsections all carry content names already, so the
# rules strip the number and let the name stand, and the cross-references name
# the content they point at.
#
# The top-level `# N. Title` headings are not flagged by the audit and keep
# their numbers.

# The 44 heading strips are NOT expressible here, and were applied as a direct
# edit instead (the commit that carries them is their archive). `load_remap`
# skips any line whose trimmed form starts with '#' as a comment, so a rule
# whose match side is a Markdown heading ('## 26.15 => ##') is read as a
# comment and silently dropped: 44 rules of that shape loaded as 35. A heading
# rename is exactly what host-lint's refusal prescribes, so the gap is worth
# closing upstream (treat a line as a comment only when it carries no ' => ').
# Stripped headings, for the record: the dotted numbers under sections 2, 3, 9,
# 26, 27 and 28, 44 of them, each keeping the content name that followed it.

# cross-references: name the content

# cross-references: name the content
